Yes, it will produce correlations. My point was to caution you that your original request to extract SEs for the correlations isn't the best idea, even if you find a way.

On Tue, Dec 15, 2020 at 5:58 PM Thi Hong Nhung Nguyen <nan...@vt.edu> wrote:

>
> Thank you, Pat.
> I think the code will produce correlation lavInspect(TR5fac.fit,"cor.lv <http://cor.lv/>") rather than covariance?
> Nhung
> On Tuesday, December 15, 2020 at 5:07:57 PM UTC-5 mal...@malonequantitative.com wrote:
> > Did a little digging -- sampling distribution of a covariance approximates normal when N is large, and we're usually using asymptotic theory in SEM anyway. Also true for modest correlations, but not for high ones, because of the cap at 1.
> >
> > On Tue, Dec 15, 2020 at 4:56 PM Patrick (Malone Quantitative) <mal...@malonequantitative.com> wrote:
> > > I don't think so, but unsolicited advice: Draw conclusions from SEs and p-values of unstandardized coefficients--in this case, covariances, not correlations. SEs on correlations aren't useful anyway, since the correlation coefficient has an asymmetric sampling distribution (a Q distribution, I think? but definitely skewed). The sampling distribution of the covariance isn't normal, either, but isn't usually going to be as bad since it doesn't have a ceiling, but there are still better ways to test it than z-tests.
> > >
> > > Pat
> > >
> > > On Tue, Dec 15, 2020 at 4:22 PM Thi Hong Nhung Nguyen <nan...@vt.edu> wrote:
