Is there a simple example of how data is derived and implemented in Lavaan

في الأحد، ٢٠ كانون الأول ٢٠٢٠ ٥:٥٧ م Patrick (Malone Quantitative) <mal...@malonequantitative.com> كتب:

> By default, the thresholds are derived from the data. Fixing them to other values will generally be a ms-specification (and harm fit).
>
> On Sun, Dec 20, 2020 at 9:52 AM Omar Salim <omarsal...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Thank you very much for the clarification
> > Yes, about the research I sent to read it, and this was my intention. If we use similar thresholds, we get a normal distribution. Are they considered hypothetical from previous studies and how are they used in Lavaan?
> >
> >    For example, are these thresholds hypothetical  𝑇𝑁(0.75, 0.05, 0.6, 0.9).or are they calculated in a specific way according to the data and how they can be used in Lavaan?
> >
> > في الأحد، 20 ديسمبر 2020 في تمام الساعة 6:00:10 ص UTC+3، كتب car...@web.de رسالة نصها:
> > > Pat, I don't think there's any disagreement here between us. It was just a comment that you can read the legendary Rhemtulla et al. study the way I wrote it: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/229154433_When_Can_Categorical_Variables_Be_Treated_as_Continuous_A_Comparison_of_Robust_Continuous_and_Categorical_SEM_Estimation_Methods_Under_Suboptimal_Conditions
> > > Am 20.12.20, 03:36 schrieb "Patrick (Malone Quantitative)" <mal...@malonequantitative.com>:
> > > > What I see as conventional wisdom is 5 points is usually enough if it's symmetric. I'm not sure it wouldn't be overtaxing MLR to apply to discrete and skewed concurrently.
> > > >
> > > > On Sat, Dec 19, 2020, 8:10 PM <car...@web.de> wrote:
> > > > > Although this is often (rightly) discussed critically, you could consider scales with 5 points as continuous and use MLR. A transformation of the data makes little sense. Pat has already noted this.
> > > > >
> > > > > Am 20.12.20, 00:58 schrieb "Patrick (Malone Quantitative)" <mal...@malonequantitative.com>:
> > > > > > The probit regression that lavaan uses with ordered data (outcomes or indicators) assumes an underlying normal distribution that is coarsely measured, at various cutpoints reflected by the thresholds.
> > > > > >
> > > > > > On Sat, Dec 19, 2020 at 6:56 PM Patrick (Malone Quantitative) <mal...@malonequantitative.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > I'm saying you shouldn't do that, even if you could.
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > On Sat, Dec 19, 2020 at 6:53 PM Omar Salim <omarsal...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > > How do you have an example for converting data into a normal distribution
> > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > ‫في الأحد، 20 ديسمبر 2020 في 2:36 ص تمت كتابة ما يلي بواسطة ‪Patrick (Malone Quantitative)‬‏ <‪mal...@malonequantitative.com‬‏>:‬
> > > > > > > > > For skewed, 5-point data, you need to use ordinal-data methods. Which lavaan can do just fine.
> > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > On Sat, Dec 19, 2020 at 6:23 PM Omar Salim <omarsal...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > > > > > Hi
> > > > > > > > > > I have five Likert scale data that are not distributed in a normal distribution. Is there a method by Lavaan that can be converted to a normal distribution?
> > > > > > > > > > I noticed that some researchers use symmetric and non-symmetric thresholds. I don’t know if it is one of the ways to transform the data into a normal distribution and how that is in Lavaan
